A seating chart is allocated in a single statement and then one cell is filled:
import java.util.Arrays;
int[][] g = new int[3][4];
g[0][0] = 7;
System.out.println((g[0] == g[1]) + " " + (g[0] == g[2]));
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(g[1]));
What does this program print?

A grid is allocated with a zero inner dimension and then measured:
int[][] g = new int[2][0];
int cells = 0;
for (int[] row : g) {
    cells += row.length;
}
System.out.println(g.length);
System.out.println(g[0] == null);
System.out.println(g[0].length);
System.out.println(cells);
What does this program print?

A report builds a two-by-three grid and then overwrites the second row with a shorter one:
int[][] g = new int[2][3];
g[1] = new int[]{7, 8};
for (int r = 0; r < g.length; r++) {
    for (int c = 0; c < g[0].length; c++) {
        System.out.print(g[r][c] + " ");
    }
}
What happens when this is compiled and run?

A builder tries to fill a jagged grid slot with a brace initializer:
int[][] g = new int[2][];
g[0] = {1, 2};
System.out.println(g[0].length);
What happens when this is compiled and run?

A jagged grid is declared and then a row of the wrong type is stored into it:
int[][] g = new int[2][];
g[0] = new double[2];
System.out.println(g[0].length);
What happens when this is compiled and run?

A programmer declares two array variables in one statement and initialises them:
int[] a, b = {5, 6};
System.out.println(b.length);
System.out.println(a.length);
What happens when this is compiled and run?

A reset routine receives a grid as a final parameter and must leave the caller holding a brand-new first row of 9s, so the row object the caller had before the call is no longer referenced:
static void load(final int[][] grid) {
    ____________
}
Which single line belongs in the blank?

A grid is written with two rows that happen to hold the same values, then one cell is edited:
import java.util.Arrays;
int[][] g = {{4, 5}, {4, 5}};
System.out.println(g[0] == g[1]);
System.out.println(Arrays.equals(g[0], g[1]));
g[0][0] = 1;
System.out.println(g[1][0]);
What does this program print?

A reader turns each line of a hand-edited file into a row and reports each row's width:
import java.util.Arrays;
String[] lines = {"7,8", "3,", ","};
for (String line : lines) {
    String[] row = line.split(",");
    System.out.println(row.length + " " + Arrays.toString(row));
}
What does this program print?

A report prints the final field of every row it was given, and one of the rows arrived empty:
String[][] rows = {{"7", "8"}, {}};
for (int r = 0; r < rows.length; r++) {
    System.out.println(rows[r][rows[r].length - 1]);
}
What happens when this is compiled and run?

A reader turns one line of a hand-edited file into a row and must keep the empty field that follows a trailing comma:
String line = "3,";
String[] row = ____________;
System.out.println(row.length);
Which expression belongs in the blank so that the program prints 2?

A grid that was declared rectangular has had one row replaced by a longer one, and a reducer measures the widest row before sweeping every column:
int[][] g = {{5, 6}, {1, 2, 3}, {9}};
int width = 0;
for (int[] row : g) {
    if (row.length > width) {
        width = row.length;
    }
}
int total = 0;
for (int r = 0; r < g.length; r++) {
    for (int c = 0; c < width; c++) {
        if (c < g[r].length) {
            total += g[r][c];
        }
    }
}
System.out.println(width + " " + total);
What does this program print?

A row is widened into a new array and both arrays are inspected:
import java.util.Arrays;
int[] row = {1, 2, 3};
int[] longer = Arrays.copyOf(row, 5);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(longer));
System.out.println(longer == row);
System.out.println(row.length);
What does this program print?

A snapshot method returns a clone of the row it is given, and the caller edits the result:
import java.util.Arrays;
static int[] snapshot(int[] row) {
    return row.clone();
}
int[] row = {1, 2, 3};
int[] snap = snapshot(row);
snap[0] = 9;
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(row) + " " + Arrays.toString(snap));
What does this program print?

A routine tries to clear the first cell of the second row by chaining an assignment onto a copy:
import java.util.Arrays;
int[][] g = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};
Arrays.copyOf(g[1], 2)[0] = 0;
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(g[1]));
What happens when this is compiled and run?

A row of names is copied and then one cell of the copy is replaced:
import java.util.Arrays;
String[][] names = {{"Ana"}, {"Ben", "Cid"}};
String[] copy = Arrays.copyOf(names[1], 2);
copy[0] = "Zed";
System.out.println(names[1][0] + " " + (copy[1] == names[1][1]));
What does this program print?

A row is shifted one place to the right by copying it onto itself:
import java.util.Arrays;
int[] row = {1, 2, 3, 4};
System.arraycopy(row, 0, row, 1, 3);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(row));
What does this program print?

A grid of string builders has one row copied, and the copy is then appended to:
import java.util.Arrays;
StringBuilder[][] cells = {{new StringBuilder("a")}, {new StringBuilder("b")}};
StringBuilder[] copy = Arrays.copyOf(cells[0], 1);
copy[0].append("!");
System.out.println(cells[0][0]);
System.out.println(cells[1][0]);
What does this program print, and what is wrong with it?

A grid is duplicated row by row and the copy is then edited, so the program must print [3, 4, 5] and then [99, 4, 5]:
import java.util.Arrays;
int[][] g = {{1, 2}, {3, 4, 5}};
int[][] copy = new int[g.length][];
for (int r = 0; r < g.length; r++) {
    ____________
}
copy[1][0] = 99;
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(g[1]));
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(copy[1]));
Which single line belongs in the blank?

A climb starts at the top-left corner and only ever steps to a larger neighbour, right or down:
int[][] g = {{1, 5, 2, 3}, {4, 6, 7, 1}, {2, 8, 9, 4}, {3, 1, 2, 10}};
int r = 0, c = 0, steps = 0;
while (true) {
    System.out.println(r + "," + c);
    int best = g[r][c];
    int nr = r, nc = c;
    if (c + 1 < g[r].length && g[r][c + 1] > best) {
        best = g[r][c + 1];
        nr = r;
        nc = c + 1;
    }
    if (r + 1 < g.length && g[r + 1][c] > best) {
        best = g[r + 1][c];
        nr = r + 1;
        nc = c;
    }
    if (nr == r && nc == c) {
        break;
    }
    r = nr;
    c = nc;
    steps++;
}
System.out.println("steps " + steps + " last " + g[r][c]);
What does this program print?

A walk climbs from the top-left, only stepping to a larger neighbour, while a separate scan measures the whole grid, and both numbers are printed:
int[][] g = {{1, 5, 2}, {2, 6, 3}, {100, 1, 4}};
int r = 0, c = 0, pathMax = g[0][0];
int globalMax = g[0][0];
for (int i = 0; i < g.length; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < g[i].length; j++) {
        if (g[i][j] > globalMax) {
            globalMax = g[i][j];
        }
    }
}
while (true) {
    if (g[r][c] > pathMax) {
        pathMax = g[r][c];
    }
    int best = g[r][c];
    int nr = r, nc = c;
    if (c + 1 < g[r].length && g[r][c + 1] > best) {
        best = g[r][c + 1];
        nr = r;
        nc = c + 1;
    }
    if (r + 1 < g.length && g[r + 1][c] > best) {
        best = g[r + 1][c];
        nr = r + 1;
        nc = c;
    }
    if (nr == r && nc == c) {
        break;
    }
    r = nr;
    c = nc;
}
System.out.println(pathMax + " " + globalMax);
What does this program print?

A walk is changed so that either neighbour test uses >= instead of >, and the grid has a tie on the top row:
int[][] g = {{1, 5, 5}, {2, 0, 9}};
int r = 0, c = 0, steps = 0;
while (true) {
    System.out.println(r + "," + c);
    int best = g[r][c];
    int nr = r, nc = c;
    if (c + 1 < g[r].length && g[r][c + 1] >= best) {
        best = g[r][c + 1];
        nr = r;
        nc = c + 1;
    }
    if (r + 1 < g.length && g[r + 1][c] >= best) {
        best = g[r + 1][c];
        nr = r + 1;
        nc = c;
    }
    if (nr == r && nc == c) {
        break;
    }
    r = nr;
    c = nc;
    steps++;
}
System.out.println("steps " + steps);
What does this program print?

A step down is guarded by a row test, and the program is run with r already on the last row of a two-row grid:
int[][] g = {{2, 3}, {5, 1}};
int r = 1, c = 0;
int best = g[r][c];
int nr = r, nc = c;
if (______________ && g[r + 1][c] > best) {
    best = g[r + 1][c];
    nr = r + 1;
    nc = c;
}
System.out.println(nr + "," + nc + " best " + best);
Which expression belongs in the blank so that this program prints 1,0 best 5?

A path is stored as row-major indexes into a three-column grid, and the row is left blank in the report:
int[][] g = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}, {7, 8, 9}};
int cols = 3;
int[] path = {0, 1, 5, 6, 8};
for (int p : path) {
    System.out.println(p + " -> " + (____) + "," + (p % cols) + " value " + g[p / cols][p % cols]);
}
Which expression belongs in the blank so that index 5 reports 1,2 with value 6?

A path is given as row-major indexes into a four-column grid, and the largest value the path touches is reported:
int[][] g = {
    {4, 9, 2, 7},
    {1, 6, 3, 8},
    {5, 2, 9, 4},
    {7, 1, 6, 3}};
int cols = 4;
int[] path = {0, 5, 10, 11, 15, 7};
int best = g[path[0] / cols][path[0] % cols];
for (int p : path) {
    if (g[p / cols][p % cols] > best) {
        best = g[p / cols][p % cols];
    }
}
System.out.println(best);
What integer is printed? Respond with the integer only.

A path is a list of cells and the routine reports the largest value along it, but the start of the path is the tallest cell:
static int maxOnPath(int[][] g, int[][] path) {
    int best = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
    for (int i = 1; i < path.length; i++) {
        int v = g[path[i][0]][path[i][1]];
        if (v > best) {
            best = v;
        }
    }
    return best;
}
int[][] g = {{9, 1, 2}, {3, 4, 5}};
int[][] path = {{0, 0}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}};
System.out.println(maxOnPath(g, path));
What is printed, and what is wrong with the routine?
